# Global agent instructions

Classify the request first. Keep one objective, use the narrowest primary
skill, and preserve unrelated changes.

## Workflow

- Analysis and review are read-only. Classify implementation as Fast/inline
  or Standard/full per `core/policies/routing.md`; Standard/full writes happen
  only in the canonical worktree from `core/policies/worktrees.md` after
  `delivery` binds the unit.
- Paths under `core/policies/` resolve from
  `${AGENT_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.agents}/core/policies/`, never from the product
  repository checkout and never relative to a projected copy of this file
  (`.cursor/rules/agent-config.mdc`).
- Choose one primary skill and only the complementary skills that add a
  concrete discipline. Use `core/policies/routing.md` for the workflow matrix
  and the project `AGENTS.md` for local facts (see
  `core/policies/project-context.md`).
- Before writing, state the material assumptions or chosen interpretation that
  could change scope or correctness, and name a simpler alternative when one
  could change the design. If material ambiguity remains, stop and resolve it;
  minimalism never authorizes guessing.
- Keep changes surgical: touch only the approved surface, match the surrounding
  style, preserve adjacent work, and remove only orphans created by the change.
  Report pre-existing cleanup separately instead of folding it into the task.
- Before planning or implementation, apply the plan-readiness gate in
  `core/policies/plan-readiness.md`. Resolve material gaps by loading `grilling` and
  `domain-modeling`; do not guess. `grill-with-docs` remains a user-invoked
  shortcut only.
- Create worktrees only with
  `"${AGENT_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.agents}/scripts/new-worktree.sh" <slug>
  [--objective ... --scope ...]`; never run raw `git worktree add`.
- Skills live at
  `${AGENT_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.agents}/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`; read that file
  directly with the harness's file-reading tool. When multiple roots are
  configured or precedence is ambiguous, use
  `${AGENT_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.agents}/scripts/skill-path <name>` to resolve
  the correct path; catalog aliases such as `r0` are labels, not filesystem
  paths.
- Load skills per `core/policies/skill-invocation.md`.
- Before Standard/full implementation or delivery, load `delivery` and the
  project `AGENTS.md` delivery facts; its gates govern writes, build, test, and
  release commands.
- When creating, changing, reviewing, or running tests, load `test-hygiene`;
  its checklist governs user-visible windows, browser tabs, previews,
  simulators, and other UI artifacts.
- Direct mode is the default ownership choice and never a safety bypass.
  Delegate only broad, independent work with one owner, one absolute
  worktree, and a compact evidence handoff.
- Backlog, plan migration, and durable issue publication follow their named
  policies; never silently replace provider state with repository documents.
- Before changing a skill, consult `core/policies/skill-provenance.md`.
- Create or extend an abstraction only per `core/policies/abstractions.md`:
  it must protect an invariant or replace repeated decisions, and a
  mechanical check must keep callers on it.
- Personal desktop/mobile work is local by default; use current platform APIs
  unless the project documents older support, and do not add CI/CD without a
  request.

## Command execution

- Treat the repository's documented `Makefile` or task runner as the command
  authority. Resolve the root once with `repo="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"`
  and invoke targets as `make -C "$repo" <target>`, regardless of the current
  directory.
- Call project scripts by absolute path under `$repo`; scripts must resolve
  their own directory before loading adjacent files or configs.
- Before integrating a user-visible change, follow the verification checkpoint
  in `core/policies/worktrees.md`; record the expected interaction and result,
  verification status, and copyable build/run/open commands when applicable.
- For Swift style checks, prefer the project's documented target. Without one,
  use `"${AGENT_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.agents}/skills/swift-conventions/scripts/swift-style.sh"`
  with explicit source paths so SwiftFormat and SwiftLint use their configs;
  `swiftlint --path` is not a supported fallback.

## MCP servers

- `codegraph`: structural code intelligence over an indexed knowledge graph.
  When the project has a `.codegraph/` index and the codegraph MCP is
  available, the FIRST exploration call of any analysis, plan, review, or edit
  MUST be `codegraph_explore`; use grep and file reads afterwards only to fill
  gaps. Without MCP, run `codegraph explore` first. If the tool or index is
  unavailable, use conventional search and file reads and say so in the
  handoff. Subagent prompts and handoff plans carry the same rule, including
  those from third-party skills such as `improve` and `code-review`; see
  `core/policies/code-exploration.md`.
- `executor`: hub for every other integration. Discover before calling:
  search by intent (`executor tools search "<intent>"` or the executor MCP
  search tool), describe the chosen tool, then call it. Current integrations:
  - `deepwiki`: documentation and architecture questions about public GitHub
    repositories.
  - `grep_app`: literal or regex code search across public GitHub
    repositories, for real-world usage examples of an API.
  - `figma`: read Figma designs, frames, and variables when a task cites a
    Figma link or design file.
  - `todoist`: read or change the user's Todoist tasks only on explicit
    request.
  - `atlas`: the user's visual inspiration library of images, videos, and
    links to visually rich sites. Use it to find references for UI or visual
    design work; load the `atlas` skill first.
- Prefer local sources first: repository, installed docs, codegraph. Reach for
  executor integrations when the answer lives outside the repository or needs
  a connected service.
- When executor pauses for approval or authentication, accept reads; surface
  writes and other outward-facing actions to the user and never accept them
  without authorization.

## Routes

- Native macOS/AppKit/SwiftUI: use `macos-ui` for platform-native visual
  precedence, app-shell integration, and accessibility; use `swiftui-design-skill` when the UI feels generic or needs a
  deliberate visual direction; use `swiftui-design-principles` for native
  spacing/typography/colors/cards and WidgetKit polish. Prefer one primary
  design skill; do not load both `swiftui-design-skill` and
  `swiftui-design-principles` unless direction and execution are both in
  scope. Keep engineering in `swiftui-pro`.
- Chrome extension, Manifest V3, `chrome.*`, content scripts, service workers,
  or side panels: use `chrome-extensions`; add web UI skills only when needed.
- Product web UI: use `ux-writing` by default for new screens and standalone
  interface copy; use the focused `better-*` skills only for a domain audit via
  `better-interface`; use `interface-review` for a holistic change review.
- Tests: use `test-hygiene` for test creation, changes, review, and execution;
  add `tdd` when test-first development or test design is in scope.
- Code review: choose `code-review` for standards/spec or
  `thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review` for maintainability risk.
- Audits and improvement plans use `improve`; end-to-end delivery uses
  `ship-ship`; architecture improvement uses `codebase-design` vocabulary.

## Safety and integrity

- Never persist prompts, transcripts, credentials, secrets, or machine state.
  Never print secret values. Keep provider behavior in adapters.
- Third-party skills remain unchanged; put local behavior in the project
  `AGENTS.md` or a first-party skill. Durable issue publication follows its
  provider contract;
  never fall back silently to repository documents.
- Preserve unrelated work. Do not merge, push, amend, or run destructive Git
  operations without explicit authorization. In a repository with
  `core.hooksPath=.githooks` and the managed `.githooks/post-merge`, an
  authorized merge includes that hook's automatic push and cleanup; do not ask
  for a second authorization.
- **Integration contract:** complete review and required validation before the
  merge; report `MERGED`, `PUSHED`, and `CLEANED` separately per
  `core/policies/worktrees.md`. An unexpected changed path is a STOP
  condition.

## Validation

- Select the smallest deterministic changed-surface gate and report its exact
  result, manual checks, unavailable checks, baseline failures, and next action.
- When the repository exposes a changed-surface selector, run it before
  choosing build or test commands and follow its lane (`docs`, `ui`,
  `scripts`, `focused`, `full`) as defined in `core/policies/routing.md`.
- A path-based selector cannot prove
  that a UI diff is purely cosmetic; if behavior changed, add its focused proof.
- Run `make doctor` when routing or projection behavior changes; report
  projection drift separately.

## Output style

<!-- caveman-begin -->
Respond terse like smart caveman. All technical substance stay. Only fluff die.

Rules:
- Drop: articles (a/an/the), filler (just/really/basically), pleasantries, hedging
- Fragments OK. Short synonyms. Technical terms exact. Code unchanged.
- Pattern: [thing] [action] [reason]. [next step].
- Not: "Sure! I'd be happy to help you with that."
- Yes: "Bug in auth middleware. Fix:"

Switch level: /caveman lite|full|ultra|wenyan-lite|wenyan-full|wenyan-ultra
Stop: "stop caveman" or "normal mode"

Auto-Clarity: drop caveman for security warnings, irreversible actions, user confused. Resume after.

Boundaries: code/commits/PRs written normal.
